{"step_index":53,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:54:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:24:35+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:24:35+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/agent-browser/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 1721\nTotal Bytes: 75173\nShowing lines 1 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # agent-browser\r\n2: \r\n3: Browser automation CLI for AI agents. Fast native Rust CLI.\r\n4: \r\n5: [![skills.sh](https://skills.sh/b/vercel-labs/agent-browser)](https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-browser)\r\n6: \r\n7: ## Installation\r\n8: \r\n9: ### Global Installation (recommended)\r\n10: \r\n11: Installs the native Rust binary:\r\n12: \r\n13: ```bash\r\n14: npm install -g agent-browser\r\n15: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n16: ```\r\n17: \r\n18: ### Project Installation (local dependency)\r\n19: \r\n20: For projects that want to pin the version in `package.json`:\r\n21: \r\n22: ```bash\r\n23: npm install agent-browser\r\n24: agent-browser install\r\n25: ```\r\n26: \r\n27: Then use via `package.json` scripts or by invoking `agent-browser` directly.\r\n28: \r\n29: ### Homebrew (macOS)\r\n30: \r\n31: ```bash\r\n32: brew install agent-browser\r\n33: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n34: ```\r\n35: \r\n36: ### Cargo (Rust)\r\n37: \r\n38: ```bash\r\n39: cargo install agent-browser\r\n40: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n41: ```\r\n42: \r\n43: ### From Source\r\n44: \r\n45: Requires Node.js 24+, pnpm 11+, and Rust.\r\n46: \r\n47: ```bash\r\n48: git clone https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser\r\n49: cd agent-browser\r\n50: pnpm install\r\n51: pnpm build\r\n52: pnpm build:native   # Requires Rust (https://rustup.rs)\r\n53: pnpm link --global  # Makes agent-browser available globally\r\n54: agent-browser install\r\n55: ```\r\n56: \r\n57: ### Linux Dependencies\r\n58: \r\n59: On Linux, \n<truncated 3207 bytes>\n             # Hover element\r\n126: agent-browser select <sel> <val>      # Select dropdown option\r\n127: agent-browser check <sel>             # Check checkbox\r\n128: agent-browser uncheck <sel>           # Uncheck checkbox\r\n129: agent-browser scroll <dir> [px]       # Scroll (up/down/left/right, --selector <sel>)\r\n130: agent-browser scrollintoview <sel>    # Scroll element into view (alias: scrollinto)\r\n131: agent-browser drag <src> <tgt>        # Drag and drop\r\n132: agent-browser upload <sel> <files>    # Upload files\r\n133: agent-browser screenshot [path]       # Take screenshot (--full for full page, saves to a temporary directory if no path)\r\n134: agent-browser screenshot --annotate   # Annotated screenshot with numbered element labels\r\n135: agent-browser screenshot --screenshot-dir ./shots    # Save to custom directory\r\n136: agent-browser screenshot --screenshot-format jpeg --screenshot-quality 80\r\n137: agent-browser pdf <path>              # Save as PDF\r\n138: agent-browser snapshot                # Accessibility tree with refs (best for AI)\r\n139: agent-browser eval <js>               # Run JavaScript (-b for base64, --stdin for piped input)\r\n140: agent-browser connect <port>          # Connect to browser via CDP\r\n141: agent-browser stream enable [--port <port>]  # Start runtime WebSocket streaming\r\n142: agent-browser stream status           # Show runtime streaming state and bound port\r\n143: agent-browser stream disable          # Stop runtime WebSocket streaming\r\n144: agent-browser close                   # Close browser (aliases: quit, exit)\r\n145: agent-browser close --all             # Close all active sessions\r\n146: agent-browser chat \"<instruction>\"    # AI chat: natural language browser control (single-shot)\r\n147: agent-browser chat                    # AI chat: interactive REPL mode\r\n148: ```\r\n149: \r\n150: ### Get Info\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":54,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:54:35Z","thinking":"**Defining Agent-Browser's Role**\n\nMy initial focus is on defining the core function of agent-browser. It's a fast Rust CLI and daemon. My current thinking revolves around how it empowers AI agents to automate and interact with web browsers efficiently. We're talking accessibility tree snapshots, references, and CDP. Right now, I am figuring out how to let agents open web pages, click elements...\n\n\n**Refining Capabilities List**\n\nI've expanded on the feature set to include filling elements, capturing annotated screenshots, evaluating JavaScript, and streaming browser sessions to reduce token overhead. I am also investigating hardware requirements. It looks like it doesn't need a dedicated GPU, it can run on CPU using headless Chrome. RAM should be around 1-2GB for Chrome and the daemon. Now, I am validating these parameters with a list of 13 other agents. I'm focusing on their summaries, GPU needs, and RAM. Finally, I will structure a clear summary and send it back.\n\n\n**Analyzing Agent Frameworks**\n\nI'm now investigating other agent frameworks to inform the design. Unsloth, a platform for LLM fine-tuning and inference, is promising for its performance gains. ComfyUI, a visual AI creation engine, also looks intriguing for its modular approach. It's becoming clear that this requires CPU and moderate RAM. The optimal environment will be 4GB-8GB system RAM. I'm focusing on hardware requirements, specifically GPU dependency.\n\n\n**Evaluating Hardware Requirements**\n\nI'm now analyzing the hardware needs for diffusion architectures. The standard generation is GPU-dependent, with CPU as a fallback. For diffusion models, I'm noting 6GB-16GB+ VRAM recommendations. The system RAM should range from 8GB to 16GB. Regarding the voice agent, Speech-to-Speech, it's CPU-compatible. However, a CUDA GPU or Apple Silicon is better for low-latency realtime interaction. For CPU use, RAM falls in the 4GB-8GB range.\n\n\n**Assessing Hardware for LLMs**\n\nMy focus is shifting to incorporating local LLMs and TTS models in pipelines. I see a need for around 6GB-12GB+ of VRAM/RAM for this. OmniVoice, the multilingual TTS model, looks promising, even if it prefers a GPU, and ViMax, a video generation framework, emphasizes API orchestration. I will analyze their hardware considerations further.\n\n\n**Determining Resource Needs**\n\nI'm now examining the memory and processing demands of graphify and Pixelle-Video. Graphify's CPU-based operation, specifically its reliance on tree-sitter AST parsing, allows for a relatively modest 2GB-4GB of RAM. Pixelle-Video, on the other hand, highlights the flexibility of a cloud-based solution, where a dedicated GPU is not necessary. Local execution of Pixelle-Video's image workflows via ComfyUI necessitates an NVIDIA GPU with 8GB-16GB+ of VRAM, making it the more resource-intensive option.\n\n\n**Estimating RAM requirements**\n\nI'm now determining RAM and VRAM necessities for video and TTS workflows. API or cloud-based applications will require at least 4-8GB system RAM, while local ComfyUI necessitates 16GB+ and 8-16GB+ respectively. Scrapling, as a CPU-based system, needs 1-2GB for HTTP requests, and 2-4GB+ for browsers. Findskill, as a lightweight tool, requires less than 512MB of system RAM.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"\"Here is the analysis of the README.md files for each of the 13 requested repositories located in `C:\\\\Users\\\\renu5\\\\Downloads\\\\find-skill-repo\\\\all github repos`:\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. **ruflo**\\n- **Summary:** Ruflo is an agent meta-harness and execution layer for Claude Code and Codex that coordinates 100+ specialized agents, swarms, and persistent vector memory (AgentDB/HNSW). It automates multi-agent collaboration, background workers, and zero-trust cross-machine agent federation without manual orchestration.\\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (Runs on CPU as a Node.js/Rust orchestration harness; optional GPU acceleration is available if routing to local models via Ollama/vLLM or RuVector GPU search).\\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~512 MB – 2 GB RAM** (standard developer environment).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. **unsloth**\\n- **Summary:** Unsloth is a high-performance LLM training, fine-tuning, and inference platform available as both a web interface (Unsloth Studio) and a Python library (Unsloth Core). It accelerates model training up to 2x–12x while cutting VRAM usage by 70%–80% across 500+ text, vision, audio, and embedding models.\\n- **Requires GPU:** **Yes** for model training and full GPU acceleration (NVIDIA RTX/Blackwell, AMD ROCm, Intel Arc, or Apple Silicon via MLX), though CPU is supported for basic chat/data recipes and GGUF inference mode.\\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~6 GB – 8 GB VRAM** for fine-tuning smaller models (16 GB – 24 GB+ VRAM for larger models); **~8 GB – 16 GB system RAM** for CPU execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. **comfyui**\\n- **Summary:** ComfyUI is a modular, node/graph-based visual AI creation engine for designing and executing generative workflows across image, video, audio, and 3D models. It offers granular pipeline control for architectures like Stable Diffusion, Flux, Wan, and Hunyuan with smart memory offloading and API support.\\n- **Requires GPU:** **Recommended/Practically Required** for standard generative workloads, though it can run on CPU using the `--cpu` flag (slow).\\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~1 GB\n<truncated 6408 bytes>","Recipient":"\"b75d3ef6-c7ea-497e-a92f-fe05edd5e5c3\"","toolAction":"\"Sending research results to caller\"","toolSummary":"\"Send README analysis results\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
